<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="plugin_camera_common_macro" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
  <!--
  Common implementation for the camera sub-elements shared by Gazebo camera sensors.

  This macro is not a complete sensor on its own.
  It is meant to be called from higher-level camera macros such as:
  - `plugin_camera_macro.xacro`
  - `plugin_camera_depth_macro.xacro`
  - `plugin_camera_rgbd_macro.xacro`

  This macro writes the common XML blocks that many Gazebo camera sensors share, including:
  - `<save>`
  - `<triggered>` and `<trigger_topic>`
  - `<camera_info_topic>`
  - `<visibility_mask>`
  - `<optical_frame_id>`
  - `<image>`
  - `<clip>`
  - `<distortion>`
  - `<lens>`
  - `<noise>`

  This macro can be used with SDFormat 1.9 and later.

  SDFormat reference:
  http://sdformat.org/spec?ver=1.11&elem=sensor#sensor_camera
  SDFormat 1.11 is parsed by `libsdformat14`, available since Gazebo Harmonic.

  Convention for parameter types:
  - `R`: real number (`float` or `double`)
  - `I`: integer
  - `S`: string
  - `B`: boolean

  Unless a suffix says otherwise, parameters use the default ROS / Gazebo units.
  For example, `_deg` means degrees, while plain angles are assumed to be in radians.

  Parameters:
  `name (S)`: unique name of the camera.

  `optical_frame (S)`: optical frame name used in the `camera_info` message header.
    Example: `color_optical_frame`.

  `save (S)`: enable or disable frame saving.
    Format: `save = 'enabled (B) path (S)'`
    - `enabled (B)`: if `true`, frame saving is enabled.
    - `path (S)`: output directory. If it is relative, it is interpreted relative to the current
      working directory.

  `triggered (B)`: if `true`, the camera is triggered by a topic instead of publishing freely.

  `namespace (S)`: namespace used to resolve relative Gazebo topics configured for the simulated
    streams. If a topic already starts with `/`, it is kept as an absolute topic.

  `trigger_topic (S)`: topic that triggers the camera when `triggered` is enabled.
     namespace is applied to this topic as well if it is relative.

  `info_topic (S)`: topic used for the `camera_info` message.
    namespace is applied to this topic as well if it is relative.

  `visible_mask (I)`: bitmask that controls which visuals this camera can see.
    A visual is rendered when:
    `(visible_mask & visual.visibility_flags) != 0`
    A value such as `0xFFFFFFFF` usually means "see everything".

  `clip (S)`: near and far clipping planes used when Gazebo renders the image.
    Format: `clip = 'near (R) far (R)'`
    - `near (R)`: near clipping distance of the camera frustum
    - `far (R)`: far clipping distance of the camera frustum

  HOW TO CHOOSE OPTICAL PARAMETERS FOR A STANDARD GNOMONICAL (PINHOLE) CAMERA:
  ============================================================================
  If you want the simulation to match a real camera, use real calibration data or manufacturer
  values for intrinsics and distortion.

  If you only need a simple ideal pinhole camera, you can derive the rest of the optical
  parameters from:
  - image width
  - image height
  - horizontal field of view

  Steps:
  - Choose `image_width_in_pixels` and `image_height_in_pixels` and pass them in `image`.

  - Given an ideal horizontal field of view, compute:
    `fx = (image_width_in_pixels * 0.5) / tan(horizontal_fov * 0.5)`
    Pass that value in `lens_intrinsics.fx`.

  - If pixels are square, which is the usual case, then `fy = fx`.
    In that case:
    `image_width_in_pixels / image_height_in_pixels = tan(0.5 * horizontal_fov) / tan(0.5 * vertical_fov)`
    so the vertical field of view becomes:
    `vertical_fov = 2 * atan((image_height_in_pixels / image_width_in_pixels) * tan(0.5 * horizontal_fov))`

  - If pixels are not square, choose a vertical field of view independently and compute:
    `fy = (image_height_in_pixels * 0.5) / tan(vertical_fov * 0.5)`
    In that case, `fx` and `fy` are computed independently and may differ.
    Pass `fy` in `lens_intrinsics.fy`.

  - Compute the principal point:
    `cx = (image_width_in_pixels - 1) * 0.5`
    `cy = (image_height_in_pixels - 1) * 0.5`

    Why `-1`?
    Because pixel indices start at 0.
    For example, a 640-pixel-wide image has indices from 0 to 639, so its center is at 319.5,
    which is `(640 - 1) / 2`.

    Pass `cx` and `cy` in `lens_intrinsics.cx` and `lens_intrinsics.cy`.

  - Compute the normalized distortion center:
    `center_x_norm = (cx + 0.5) / image_width_in_pixels`
    `center_y_norm = (cy + 0.5) / image_height_in_pixels`

    Why `+0.5`?
    Because the distortion center is expressed in edge coordinates, while `cx` and `cy` refer to
    pixel centers.

    Pass these values in `distortion_center`.

  - If you know the lens distortion coefficients `k1`, `k2`, `k3`, `p1`, and `p2`, set them in
    `distortion_k_p`.
    If you want an ideal pinhole camera, set all of them to zero.

  `image (S)`: image size and format.
    Format: `image = 'width (I) height (I) format (S) anti_aliasing (I)'`
    - `width (I)`: image width in pixels
    - `height (I)`: image height in pixels
    - `format (S)`: one of:
      `L8 | L16 | R8G8B8 | B8G8R8 | BAYER_RGGB8 | BAYER_BGGR8 | BAYER_GBRG8 | BAYER_GRBG8 | R_FLOAT16 | R_FLOAT32`

      This format controls the image stored in the `<image>` block of the Gazebo camera model.
      Higher-level macros decide whether that `<image>` block becomes a published color topic or is
      only used internally by Gazebo.

      If you choose `R8G8B8`, the image is RGB.
      If you choose `L8`, the image is monochrome.
      Float formats are single-channel floating-point images and are usually only meaningful for
      special use cases.

    - `anti_aliasing (I)`: anti-aliasing level

  `lens_single_elements (S)`: single-element lens model.
    Format:
    `lens_single_elements = 'type (S) scale_to_hfov (B) cutoff_angle_deg (R) env_texture_size (I)'`
    - `type (S)`: one of `gnomonical`, `stereographic`, `equidistant`, `equisolid_angle`,
      `orthographic`, or `custom`
      - `gnomonical`: standard pinhole projection
      - `stereographic`: preserves angles, common in fisheye models
      - `equidistant`: radial distance is proportional to incidence angle
      - `equisolid_angle`: image area is proportional to solid angle
      - `orthographic`: no perspective effects
      - `custom`: uses the function defined in `lens_custom_function`
    - `scale_to_hfov (B)`: if `true`, the image is scaled to match the horizontal field of view
    - `cutoff_angle_deg (R)`: anything beyond this angle is hidden
    - `env_texture_size (I)`: cube-map resolution used by Gazebo for rendering the environment

  `lens_custom_function (S)`: custom mapping function used only when
    `lens_single_elements.type == 'custom'`.
    Format:
    `lens_custom_function = 'c1 (R) c2 (R) c3 (R) f (R) fun (S)'`
    - `c1`, `c2`, `c3`: mapping constants
    - `f (R)`: optical-system focal-length-like parameter
    - `fun (S)`: one of `tan`, `sin`, or `id`

  `lens_intrinsics (S)`: intrinsic camera matrix parameters.
    Format:
    `lens_intrinsics = 'fx (R) fy (R) cx (R) cy (R)'`
    These values form the intrinsic matrix:

      `K = | fx  s  cx |`
      `    |  0 fy  cy |`
      `    |  0  0   1 |`

    where `s` is `lens_xy_skew`.
    If lens_projection is also provided, it takes precedence over `lens_intrinsics`.

  `lens_xy_skew (R)`: skew term of the intrinsic matrix.
    In almost all modern cameras this is `0.0`. Non-zero values are only needed for unusual or
    imperfect sensors.

  `lens_projection (S)`: projection matrix parameters that override the intrinsic matrix when
    needed, for example in stereo setups where projection and intrinsics differ.
    Format:
    `lens_projection = 'p_fx p_fy p_cx p_cy tx ty'`
    - `p_fx (R)`: projection focal length in x
    - `p_fy (R)`: projection focal length in y
    - `p_cx (R)`: projection principal point in x
    - `p_cy (R)`: projection principal point in y
    - `tx (R)`: translation term in x
    - `ty (R)`: translation term in y
    If you use `lens_projection`, `lens_intrinsics` is ignored.

  `distortion_k_p (S)`: lens distortion coefficients.
    Format:
    `distortion_k_p = 'k1 (R) k2 (R) k3 (R) p1 (R) p2 (R)'`
    - `k1`, `k2`, `k3`: radial distortion coefficients
    - `p1`, `p2`: tangential distortion coefficients

    If all of them are zero, the image has no optical distortion. That is the standard choice for
    an ideal pinhole camera.

  `distortion_center (S)`: normalized coordinates of the distortion center.
    Format:
    `distortion_center = 'center_x_norm (R) center_y_norm (R)'`
    Both values are expected in the range `[0.0, 1.0]`.

  `gaussian_noise (S)`: Gaussian noise model applied to generated images.
    Format:
    `gaussian_noise = 'mean (R) stddev (R)'`
    - `mean (R)`: Gaussian mean
    - `stddev (R)`: Gaussian standard deviation
  -->
  <xacro:macro name="plugin_camera_common"
               params="name
                       optical_frame
                       save:='False /tmp'
                       triggered:=False
                       visible_mask:=4294967295
                       clip:='0.1 100.0'
                       image
                       lens_single_elements:='gnomonical true 90.0 256'
                       lens_custom_function:=''
                       lens_intrinsics
                       lens_xy_skew
                       lens_projection
                       distortion_k_p:=''
                       distortion_center:='0.5 0.5'
                       gaussian_noise:='0.0 0.0'
                       namespace:=''
                       info_topic
                       trigger_topic:=''">

    <xacro:include filename="$(find robotics_description)/urdf/common/resolve_topic_macro.xacro"/>

    <xacro:property name="save_seq"                 value="${save.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(save_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid save parameter format. Expected format: 'enabled (B) path (S)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="save_enabled"             value="${save_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="save_path"                value="${save_seq[1]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="clip_seq"                 value="${clip.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(clip_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid clip parameter format. Expected format: 'near (R) far (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="near_clip"                value="${clip_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="far_clip"                 value="${clip_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_seq"                value="${image.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(image_seq) != 4}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid image parameter format. Expected format: 'width (I) height (I) format (S) anti_aliasing (I)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="image_width_px"           value="${image_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_height_px"          value="${image_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_format"             value="${image_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="image_anti_aliasing"      value="${image_seq[3]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="lens_single_elements_seq" value="${lens_single_elements.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(lens_single_elements_seq) != 4}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_single_elements parameter format. Expected format: 'type (S) scale_to_hfov (B) cutoff_angle_deg (R) env_texture_size (I)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="lens_type"                value="${lens_single_elements_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_scale_to_hfov"       value="${lens_single_elements_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_cutoff_angle_deg"    value="${lens_single_elements_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="lens_env_texture_size"    value="${lens_single_elements_seq[3]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="distortion_k_p_seq"       value="${distortion_k_p.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(distortion_k_p_seq) != 5}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid distortion_k_p parameter format. Expected format: 'k1 (R) k2 (R) k3 (R) p1 (R) p2 (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="k1"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="k2"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[1]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="k3"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[2]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="p1"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[3]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="p2"                       value="${distortion_k_p_seq[4]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="distortion_center_seq"    value="${distortion_center.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(distortion_center_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid distortion_center parameter format. Expected format: 'center_x_norm (R) center_y_norm (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="center_norm"              value="${distortion_center_seq[0]} ${distortion_center_seq[1]}"/>

    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_seq"       value="${gaussian_noise.split()}"/>

    <xacro:if value="${len(gaussian_noise_seq) != 2}">
      ${xacro.fatal("Invalid gaussian_noise parameter format. Expected format: 'mean (R) stddev (R)'")}
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_mean"      value="${gaussian_noise_seq[0]}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gaussian_noise_stddev"    value="${gaussian_noise_seq[1]}"/>

    <save enabled="${save_enabled}">
      <path>${save_path}</path>
    </save>

    <triggered>${triggered}</triggered>

    <xacro:if value="${triggered}">
      <xacro:resolve_topic prop_id="resolved_trigger_topic"
                          topic="${trigger_topic}"
                          namespace="${namespace}"/>

      <trigger_topic>${resolved_trigger_topic}</trigger_topic>
    </xacro:if>

    <xacro:resolve_topic prop_id="resolved_info_topic"
                         topic="${info_topic}"
                         namespace="${namespace}"/>

    <camera_info_topic>${resolved_info_topic}</camera_info_topic>

    <visibility_mask>${visible_mask}</visibility_mask>

    <optical_frame_id>${optical_frame}</optical_frame_id>

    <image>
      <width>${image_width_px}</width>
      <height>${image_height_px}</height>
      <format>${image_format}</format>
      <anti_aliasing>${image_anti_aliasing}</anti_aliasing>
    </image>

    <clip>
      <near>${near_clip}</near>
      <far>${far_clip}</far>
    </clip>

    <distortion>
      <k1>${k1}</k1>
      <k2>${k2}</k2>
      <k3>${k3}</k3>
      <p1>${p1}</p1>
      <p2>${p2}</p2>
      <center>${center_norm}</center>
    </distortion>

    <lens>
      <type>${lens_type}</type>
      <scale_to_hfov>${lens_scale_to_hfov}</scale_to_hfov>
      <cutoff_angle>${radians(float(lens_cutoff_angle_deg))}</cutoff_angle>
      <env_texture_size>${lens_env_texture_size}</env_texture_size>

      <!--
      If lens_type is 'custom', use the 'lens_custom_function' parameter to define the 'custom_function' element.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_type == 'custom'}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_custom_function_seq" value="${lens_custom_function.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_custom_function_seq) != 5}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_custom_function parameter format. Expected format: 'c1 (R) c2 (R) c3 (R) f (R) fun (S)'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="c1"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="c2"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="c3"  value="${lens_custom_function_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="f"   value="${lens_custom_function_seq[3]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="fun" value="${lens_custom_function_seq[4]}"/>

        <custom_function>
          <c1>${c1}</c1>
          <c2>${c2}</c2>
          <c3>${c3}</c3>
          <f>${f}</f>
          <fun>${fun}</fun>
        </custom_function>
      </xacro:if>

      <!--
      According to the sdformat documentation, if lens_projection is defined, it overrides the intrinsics parameters
      fx, fy, cx, cy.
      Therefore, if lens_projection is defined, it is used.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_projection != ''}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_projection_seq" value="${lens_projection.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_projection_seq) != 6}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_projection parameter format. Expected format: 'p_fx p_fy p_cx p_cy tx ty'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="p_fx" value="${lens_projection_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_fy" value="${lens_projection_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_cx" value="${lens_projection_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="p_cy" value="${lens_projection_seq[3]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="tx"   value="${lens_projection_seq[4]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="ty"   value="${lens_projection_seq[5]}"/>

        <projection>
          <p_fx>${p_fx}</p_fx>
          <p_fy>${p_fy}</p_fy>
          <p_cx>${p_cx}</p_cx>
          <p_cy>${p_cy}</p_cy>
          <tx>${tx}</tx>
          <ty>${ty}</ty>
        </projection>

        <!--
        When 'lens_projection' is used, still set the 'lens_xy_skew' in the intrinsics element since there is no other
        way to set it.
        Since the rest of the element in intrinsics are required (see sdformat documentation), they must be set,
        otherwise sdf parser will raise an error. However, in practical terms, no matter the values assigned to fx, fy,
        cx, cy here, they will be overridden by the values in projection (p_fx, p_fy, p_cx, p_cy), as described in the
        sdformat documentation.
        Therefore, the parameters fx, fy, cx, cy are set to p_fx, p_fy, p_cx, p_cy respectively, just to avoid errors
        in sdf parsing, knowing that these values will be overridden later by the projection values.
        -->
        <intrinsics>
          <fx>${p_fx}</fx>
          <fy>${p_fy}</fy>
          <cx>${p_cx}</cx>
          <cy>${p_cy}</cy>
          <s>${lens_xy_skew}</s>
        </intrinsics>
      </xacro:if>

      <!--
      If `lens_projection` is not defined, use `lens_intrinsics` to populate the `<intrinsics>` element.
      The presence or absence of `custom_function` does not change this part.
      -->
      <xacro:if value="${lens_projection == ''}">
        <xacro:property name="lens_intrinsics_seq" value="${lens_intrinsics.split()}"/>

        <xacro:if value="${len(lens_intrinsics_seq) != 4}">
          ${xacro.fatal("Invalid lens_intrinsics parameter format. Expected format: 'fx (R) fy (R) cx (R) cy (R)'")}
        </xacro:if>

        <xacro:property name="fx"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[0]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="fy"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[1]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="cx"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[2]}"/>
        <xacro:property name="cy"  value="${lens_intrinsics_seq[3]}"/>

        <intrinsics>
          <fx>${fx}</fx>
          <fy>${fy}</fy>
          <cx>${cx}</cx>
          <cy>${cy}</cy>
          <s>${lens_xy_skew}</s>
        </intrinsics>
      </xacro:if>
    </lens>

    <noise>
      <type>gaussian</type>
      <mean>${gaussian_noise_mean}</mean>
      <stddev>${gaussian_noise_stddev}</stddev>
    </noise>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
